Two decisions actually fork this:

**Key by `client_id`, not IP.** The auth middleware already resolves `req.client.client_id` from the API key before your routes run (`routes/api.js`) — and the comment above it notes several clients can share one IP behind NAT, so IP-based limiting would throttle innocent tenants alongside the offender.

**Count in Redis, not in-process.** `app.js` runs 4 clustered workers per box with nothing shared in module scope except Redis (`redis.js` says as much) — an in-memory counter only sees a quarter of one box's traffic, so the limit would be ~4x too loose and inconsistent across boxes. Redis is already the shared store, so reuse it.

Recommendation: add a check at the top of the existing auth middleware in `routes/api.js`, right after `req.client` is set, before any DB query runs:

```js
router.use(async (req, res, next) => {
  const key = req.get("X-Api-Key");
  if (!key) return res.status(401).end();
  const rows = await db.query(
    "SELECT client_id, plan FROM api_keys WHERE key = $1 AND revoked_at IS NULL",
    [key]
  );
  if (!rows.length) return res.status(401).end();
  req.client = rows[0];

  const limit = PLAN_LIMITS[req.client.plan]; // requests per window
  const windowKey = `rl:${req.client.client_id}:${Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000 / WINDOW_SECONDS)}`;
  const count = await redis.client.incr(windowKey);
  if (count === 1) await redis.client.expire(windowKey, WINDOW_SECONDS);
  if (count > limit) {
    res.set("Retry-After", String(WINDOW_SECONDS));
    return res.status(429).end();
  }

  next();
});
```

This is a fixed-window counter (`INCR` + `EXPIRE` on a per-client, per-window key) — one round trip per request, atomic enough for this purpose since `INCR` is a single Redis command. It allows a burst at window boundaries (up to 2x limit across a boundary); a token bucket via a Lua script smooths that out at the cost of a bit more code. For "one client hammering everyone else," fixed-window is enough — swap in a bucket later only if you see boundary bursts causing pain in practice.

Left out: per-plan limit values, whether to rate-limit by endpoint (POST vs GET) separately, and alerting/observability on 429 rates — next-level decisions once the basic gate is in place.

At 38k rows on Postgres 16, use Postgres full-text search, not a separate search engine like Elasticsearch — that's a real fork this scale settles, not one to leave open.

Add a generated `tsvector` column over `name` and `description` (weight `name` higher with `setweight`), index it with GIN, and query with `websearch_to_tsquery` so users can type free-text words:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search_vector tsvector
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
  ) STORED;

CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING GIN (search_vector);
```

New route, same shape as the existing paged listing in `routes/products.js`:

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = (req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents,
            ts_rank(search_vector, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)) AS rank
     FROM products
     WHERE active AND search_vector @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)
     ORDER BY rank DESC
     LIMIT 50`,
    [q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

